  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 332 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\n...TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT...\n\nModest but persistent mid-level WAA across northern and eastern \nKansas has provided enough ascent to support the development of \nscattered showers and storms generally along and northeast of a \nSalina-Eureka-Chanute line early this morning. Meanwhile, a loosely \norganized broken line of showers and storms is quickly progressing \neastward across western Kansas, and has had a history of producing \ngusty winds between 40-50 mph. As the initial activity slowly exits \neastward, this second round of showers and storms will be arriving \nlater this morning; however, the intensity of this activity is \nexpected to decrease given widespread robust convective inhibition \nacross much of the forecast area. In general, chances for showers \nand storms will gradually end from west to east later this morning \ninto early this afternoon. It's possible some showers and storms \nwill continue to linger across far eastern Kansas through much of \nthe day, but much of this activity during the afternoon hours \nshould be limited to Missouri.\n\nModel guidance over the past few days has consistently shown a sharp \ndeep layer trough digging into the northern and central plains later \ntonight. Based on the short range guidance this morning, not \nmuch has changed. At the same time, a weak cold front will push \nsoutheastward across the northern and central plains. Given \nbroad forcing for ascent from the incoming trough and frontal \nboundary moving into a ridiculously moist airmass (PWATs well \nover 2 inches), it's all but certain a line of storms will \ndevelop along the cold front and sweep across much of the region\nlate tonight into Friday morning. While it's possible this line\nof storms will produce strong to marginally severe wind gusts, \nthe main factor curbing storm intensity will be extensive \nconvective inhibition for surface based parcels. However, \nelevated instability will remain uncapped overnight, and given a\nrobust LLJ, these storms should remain intact and organized \nthrough the overnight hours. Any severe wind gusts that occur \nshould remain isolated though due to the surface based CIN. By \nFriday morning, most of the convection should be well off to the\nsouth and east. In the wake of this activity, northerly surface\nwinds will overspread the region which should help to moderate \ntemperatures somewhat. However, it remains possible that some \nlocations across southern Kansas could see afternoon highs in \nthe mid 90s. The primary surge of cooler air is forecast to \narrive Friday night which should help drop temperatures into \nthe 60s across the forecast area.\n\n...THIS WEEKEND THROUGH THE BEGINNING OF NEXT WEEK...\n\nMid range deterministic and ensemble model guidance continues to \nshow a pattern that favors cooler air across the region this \nweekend with broad upper troughing across the eastern CONUS and \na strong upper ridge over the Desert Southwest. At the surface, \na robust surface ridge across the northern plains/Midwest will \nsupport at least a couple of days of northerly low level flow. \nSaturday in particular could be quite breezy given a tight \npressure gradient across the area. These northerly winds will \nserve to bring cooler temperatures into the region, especially \nacross eastern Kansas where many locations may only see highs in\nthe upper 70s to low 80s Saturday afternoon! That being said, \ntemperatures will begin to gradually rebound beginning on Sunday\nas modest mid/upper ridging slowly builds in across the central\nplains. By mid-week next week, temperatures should return to \nnear, or just a tick above average. In addition to the brief \ncool down, drier air will overspread the region in the wake of \nFriday's cold front. Therefore, rain chances this weekend and \ninto next week will be minute at best.",
